    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a deck build in Vancouver and need to double check my math on the load requirements. It's a cedar deck attached to a single-story house. Can you explain the difference between live load and dead load for me and how I should be approaching the calculations for post spacing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In deck construction, live load (LL) and dead load (DL) are two critical factors to consider when calculating load requirements. Dead load refers to the weight of the deck itself, including the wood, fasteners, and other materials, while live load accounts for the weight of users, furniture, and other external loads. When calculating post spacing, you should approach it by adding the tradeoff factor for live load (usually 1.5 to 2 times LL) and the minimum Code factor (1 or 1.5) to the DL to determine the total load. This will give you the required post spacing to support the combined loads.",
      "source": "target",
